---
import Shell from '../layouts/Shell.astro';
import sources from '../data/sources.json';
import provenance from '../data/provenance.json';
const retrieved = new Date(provenance.retrievedAt).toLocaleDateString('en-GB', { day: 'numeric', month: 'long', year: 'numeric', timeZone: 'UTC' });
---
<Shell title="Sources & methodology" active="sources"><article class="prose"><span class="eyebrow">Follow the reference</span><h1>Sources & methodology</h1><p>Each Quran Connections puzzle shows three Quran passages that share one theme, with a commentary link for each passage.</p><h2>What this app quotes</h2><p>Quran text comes from <a href="https://mcp.quran.ai" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Quran Foundation’s official Quran MCP <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a> and is bundled with the app, so it works without a live connection.</p><ul><li>Arabic: Uthmani Hafs, <code>{provenance.quranEdition}</code>, preserved exactly as retrieved.</li><li>English: Marmaduke Pickthall, <code>{provenance.translationEdition}</code>. His historical public-domain wording is kept rather than silently modernised. A translation is an interpretation of meaning.</li></ul><h2>Commentary linked</h2><p>Commentary is linked, not republished. Short English notes are our own paraphrases checked against the retrieved Arabic.</p><ul><li>Al-Tafsir al-Muyassar, the concise Arabic commentary prepared by a committee at the King Fahd Quran Printing Complex (<code>ar-muyassar</code>)</li></ul><h2>What is editorial</h2><p>Themes, answer options, distractors and difficulty are editorial. A matching answer identifies the intended puzzle answer, not the only possible observation about the passages. None of it is a ruling or an opinion from quran.ai, Quran.com or Quran Foundation, and no independent scholarly review is claimed.</p><h2>Limits</h2><p>A puzzle shows three passages. The Quran treats every theme in many more places.</p><h2>Snapshot and reuse</h2><p>Canonical snapshot retrieved on {retrieved}. Source records, checksums and edition names ship with the project. Original application code is MIT licensed. DM Sans, Newsreader and Amiri are hosted locally under the SIL Open Font License, with their licence files included.</p><p class="micro">Snapshot SHA-256: <code style="overflow-wrap:anywhere">{provenance.snapshotSha256}</code><br />App content SHA-256: <code style="overflow-wrap:anywhere">{provenance.appContentSha256}</code></p><h2>References used in this app</h2><p>{sources.length} linked sources. Each opens the source provider in a new tab.</p><ul class="source-list">{sources.map(source=><li><a href={source.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">{source.label} <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a> <small>{source.kind}{source.note ? ` · ${source.note}` : ''}</small></li>)}</ul><div class="actions top-gap"><a class="button" href="/">Return to the activity <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a> <button class="button secondary" data-print>Print source notes</button></div></article></Shell>
